Lab Overview
Prometheus is an open-source monitoring and alerting toolkit designed for reliability and scalability. It collects metrics from configured targets at given intervals, evaluates rule expressions, displays results, and can trigger alerts when specified conditions are observed.

Key Components of Prometheus:
Prometheus Server: Scrapes and stores time series data
Client Libraries: For instrumenting application code
Push Gateway: For supporting short-lived jobs
Exporters: For exposing metrics from third-party systems
Alertmanager: For handling alerts
Task 1: Install Prometheus
Step 1.1: Update System Packages
First, ensure your system is up to date:

sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y
Step 1.2: Create Prometheus User
Create a dedicated user for running Prometheus:

sudo useradd --no-create-home --shell /bin/false prometheus
Step 1.3: Create Required Directories
Create directories for Prometheus configuration and data:

sudo mkdir /etc/prometheus
sudo mkdir /var/lib/prometheus
Set proper ownership:

sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /var/lib/prometheus
Step 1.4: Download and Install Prometheus
Navigate to the temporary directory and download Prometheus:

cd /tmp
wget https://github.com/prometheus/prometheus/releases/download/v2.47.0/prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz
Extract the downloaded archive:

tar xvf prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64.tar.gz
cd prometheus-2.47.0.linux-amd64
Copy the binaries to the appropriate locations:

sudo cp prometheus /usr/local/bin/
sudo cp promtool /usr/local/bin/
Set proper ownership and permissions:

sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /usr/local/bin/prometheus
sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /usr/local/bin/promtool
Copy configuration files:

sudo cp -r consoles /etc/prometheus
sudo cp -r console_libraries /etc/prometheus
Set ownership for configuration directories:

sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/consoles
sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/console_libraries
Step 1.5: Verify Installation
Check if Prometheus is installed correctly:

prometheus --version
You should see output similar to:

prometheus, version 2.47.0 (branch: HEAD, revision: 7f8e09f)
Task 2: Configure Prometheus Server
Step 2.1: Create Prometheus Configuration File
Create the main configuration file for Prometheus:

sudo nano /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Add the following configuration:

global:
  scrape_interval: 15s
  evaluation_interval: 15s

rule_files:
  # - "first_rules.yml"
  # - "second_rules.yml"

scrape_configs:
  - job_name: 'prometheus'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9090']

  - job_name: 'node_exporter'
    static_configs:
      - targets: ['localhost:9100']
Save and exit the file (Ctrl+X, then Y, then Enter).

Step 2.2: Set Configuration File Ownership
Set proper ownership for the configuration file:

sudo chown prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Step 2.3: Create Systemd Service File
Create a systemd service file for Prometheus:

sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/prometheus.service
Add the following content:

[Unit]
Description=Prometheus
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=prometheus
Group=prometheus
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/prometheus \
    --config.file /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml \
    --storage.tsdb.path /var/lib/prometheus/ \
    --web.console.templates=/etc/prometheus/consoles \
    --web.console.libraries=/etc/prometheus/console_libraries \
    --web.listen-address=0.0.0.0:9090 \
    --web.enable-lifecycle

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
Save and exit the file.

Step 2.4: Install and Configure Node Exporter
Download Node Exporter to provide system metrics:

cd /tmp
wget https://github.com/prometheus/node_exporter/releases/download/v1.6.1/node_exporter-1.6.1.linux-amd64.tar.gz
Extract and install:

tar xvf node_exporter-1.6.1.linux-amd64.tar.gz
sudo cp node_exporter-1.6.1.linux-amd64/node_exporter /usr/local/bin/
Create a user for Node Exporter:

sudo useradd --no-create-home --shell /bin/false node_exporter
sudo chown node_exporter:node_exporter /usr/local/bin/node_exporter
Create a systemd service for Node Exporter:

sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/node_exporter.service
Add the following content:

[Unit]
Description=Node Exporter
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
User=node_exporter
Group=node_exporter
Type=simple
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/node_exporter

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
Save and exit the file.

Task 3: Verify Prometheus is Collecting Metrics
Step 3.1: Start Services
Reload systemd and start the services:

sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start node_exporter
sudo systemctl start prometheus
Enable services to start on boot:

sudo systemctl enable node_exporter
sudo systemctl enable prometheus
Step 3.2: Check Service Status
Verify that both services are running:

sudo systemctl status prometheus
sudo systemctl status node_exporter
Both services should show active (running) status.

Step 3.3: Verify Port Accessibility
Check if Prometheus and Node Exporter are listening on their respective ports:

sudo netstat -tlnp | grep :9090
sudo netstat -tlnp | grep :9100
You should see both ports in the LISTEN state.

Step 3.4: Test Metrics Collection
Test if Node Exporter is providing metrics:

curl http://localhost:9100/metrics | head -20
You should see various system metrics being exposed.

Test if Prometheus is accessible:

curl http://localhost:9090/api/v1/targets
This should return JSON data showing the configured targets.

Step 3.5: Access Prometheus Web Interface
Since you're working on a single machine, you can use a text-based browser to verify the web interface:

sudo apt install -y lynx
lynx http://localhost:9090
Navigate through the interface using arrow keys. You should see:

The Prometheus web interface
Status > Targets page showing both prometheus and node_exporter targets as UP
Step 3.6: Query Metrics Using PromQL
Test some basic PromQL queries using curl:

Query to check Prometheus server uptime:

curl -G http://localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode 'query=up'
Query to check CPU usage:

curl -G http://localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode 'query=100 - (avg by (instance) (rate(node_cpu_seconds_total{mode="idle"}[5m])) * 100)'
Query to check memory usage:

curl -G http://localhost:9090/api/v1/query --data-urlencode 'query=node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes'
Step 3.7: Verify Data Storage
Check if Prometheus is storing data:

sudo ls -la /var/lib/prometheus/
You should see directories and files created by Prometheus for storing time-series data.

Step 3.8: View Configuration
Verify your configuration is loaded correctly:

curl http://localhost:9090/api/v1/status/config
This will show the current configuration that Prometheus is using.

Troubleshooting Tips
Common Issues and Solutions:
Service fails to start:

Check service logs: sudo journalctl -u prometheus -f
Verify file permissions and ownership
Check configuration file syntax: promtool check config /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Targets showing as DOWN:

Verify services are running: sudo systemctl status node_exporter
Check if ports are accessible: netstat -tlnp | grep 9100
Review firewall settings if applicable
Permission denied errors:

Ensure proper ownership: sudo chown -R prometheus:prometheus /etc/prometheus/
Check directory permissions: ls -la /var/lib/prometheus/
Configuration errors:

Validate YAML syntax: promtool check config /etc/prometheus/prometheus.yml
Check indentation in YAML files (use spaces, not tabs)
